 ],
 "fieldNotes": "SAMPLE (this is what a voice memo transcript looks like when it comes in):\nLeft Spooner around ten past eight. Cold, maybe 40. Climb up North Canyon was steady, granny gear most of the way. Saw two other bikes and a guy walking a husky. Marlette was flat calm, could see the bottom near the shore. Found a fallen pinyon branch with good clean resin near the top, took a little for the lamp project. Fast way down, legs done. Forgot to stop Cyclemeter again, it's got the drive home on it.",
 "post": {
  "title": "Up to Marlette Lake",
  "body": "The climb out of Spooner Lake starts gently and then stops pretending. North Canyon is a steady pull for most of the way up, and this morning it was a cold one, around 40 degrees at the trailhead.\n\nMarlette Lake was flat calm at the top: clear enough to see the bottom near the shore. Two other riders and a hiker with a husky were the only company.\n\nNear the top, a fallen pinyon branch had clean, honey-coloured resin on it. A small piece came home for the LED lamp project (a note on collecting: small amounts for personal use, from fallen wood, and check the land manager's rules first).\n\nWhat I learned: the granny gear is not a defeat. And stop the tracker before you get in the car."
 },
 "episode": {
  "title": "Episode sample: The resin at the top of the hill",
  "script": "It's ten past eight at Spooner Lake, and it's cold. Forty degrees, give or take, and Christian is already clipping in.\n\nThe trail up North Canyon doesn't announce itself. It just keeps going up. He settles into the lowest gear on the bike and stays there, and honestly? That's the plan. There's no prize for suffering faster.\n\n***\n\nMarlette Lake, when he gets there, is perfectly still. The kind of still where you can see the stones on the bottom near the shore. A couple of other riders roll through. A man walks by with a husky that seems to approve of all of this.\n\nAnd then, near the top, a fallen pinyon branch. Christian stops. On the bark there's a bead of resin the colour of honey, and he takes a small piece home. It's for a lamp he's been building: resin, a little light behind it, and a gift for someone who doesn't know it yet.\n\n***\n\nThe way down is fast and loud and over too soon. And at the bottom, the one thing he forgets every single time: to stop the tracker before the drive home.\n\nHe'll remember next time. Probably.",
  "audio": null
 },
 "sample": true
}
